{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"sainted-regardable-burro","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Context / Problem\n\nThis store's enrichment queue holds **898 pending entries across 4,903 tickets**\n(measured 2026-08-21). `enrich_drain.drain` processes at most `overlap_drain_batch`\n(default 5) SUCCESSES per run, and `overlap.queue.pending_enrichment` returns ids **sorted**,\nso each run works a fixed prefix of the same sorted list. Entries late in sort order are\nreached only once everything ahead of them drains.\n\nTwo consequences, both observable today:\n\n**1. Late-sorted entries starve.** The three tickets from bug `569c-931f-69a2-4c1d` sit at\nsorted indices **458 / 499 / 786**. Even with that bug fixed — their prompts now bound\ncorrectly and they enrich in ~8 s against the real provider — they will not reach `done`\nuntil the ~458-deep backlog ahead of them clears.\n\n**2. The O(1) gate marker can never engage, so every store write pays a full scan.**\n`pending_enrichment` clears the gate marker whenever it finds anything pending\n(`queue.py:374-375`). With 898 entries permanently pending, the marker is cleared on every\nprobe, `958e-f4b3-275c-4154`'s fast path is unreachable on this store, and ordinary writes\nlog:\n\n```\nWARNING rebar.llm.enrich_drain: enrich drain gate check took ~900-1200 ms (> 20 ms budget)\n```\n\nThat warning fires on essentially **100% of writes** here. It is frequently misattributed to\nwhichever enrichment bug is currently open; the actual dominant cause is the backlog depth.\n\n## Open questions for whoever claims this\n- Is 898 a transient backlog draining down after bug `3198-438c-72a5-470f` (detached drain\n  children dying at `getcwd()`) was fixed, or is enqueue outpacing a 5-per-write drain?\n  Measure the trend before changing anything.\n- If it is steady-state, the batch cap and/or the sorted iteration order are the levers:\n  sorted order makes starvation deterministic rather than fair. Oldest-`not_before_ns` first,\n  or a rotating start offset, would at least make it uniform.\n- Should the gate marker record \"nothing pending BEFORE index N\" rather than being all-or-\n  nothing, so a deep backlog does not permanently disable the fast path?\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] The backlog trend is measured and reported (draining vs steady-state vs growing).\n- [ ] No queue entry can be starved indefinitely by sort position while other entries drain.\n- [ ] On a store with a deep backlog, the write-path gate check is back inside its budget (or\n      the budget/warning is re-justified for that regime).\n- [ ] `make lint`, `make typecheck` and CI's pytest invocation are green.\n\n## Provenance\nFound while debugging `569c-931f-69a2-4c1d`. It was tested and REJECTED as that bug's root\ncause — the three entries were observed being claimed and failing the provider 400 on demand,\nwhich starvation alone cannot explain — but it is a real independent problem, and it is the\nreason that bug's AC3 (\"the three entries reach `done` on this store\") is not satisfied by\nthat bug's fix alone.","id":"f400-987f-45f6-419a","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"deep enrichment backlog starves late entries"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1787297347664581001,"uuid":"d5ed4855-0076-41cb-8d57-f2a5c3b2105a"}